    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Two important findings before the options: your catalog files (`basic_fil_data.csv`, `fil_data.csv`) currently only have `start_time`, `hemisphere`, `chirality` \u2014 no coordinates at all, so step zero is pulling filament location from whatever catalog you sourced this from (HEK or similar). And I checked `embedding.py`: the position encoding is an **analytic Fourier function of `img_size`/`patch_size`**, not a learned lookup table, so it can't just be sliced or interpolated \u2014 changing `img_size` regenerates it with a different frequency basis than what the pretrained backbone learned on. That constrains the crop options below.\n\n**A. Add a location-prior channel (recommended starting point).** Bake a Gaussian heatmap or bounding box centered on the filament's pixel location into a 14th input channel, alongside the existing 13. Keeps `img_size=4096` and the position encoding untouched \u2014 the pretrained checkpoint loads fine except the patch-embed conv, whose new channel-slice you zero-init so the model starts out identical to pretrained behavior on the original 13. This is the standard \"prompt/hint channel\" trick (same idea as SAM's point prompts) \u2014 cheap, doesn't touch backbone math, and only tells the model *where*, not *what the answer is*.\n\n**B. Crop + resize back to full size (\"zoom\").** Use the coordinate to crop a smaller window (say 512\u20131024 px) around the filament, then upsample back to 4096\u00d74096 before feeding it in. Pure dataset-side change, zero architecture change, since `img_size` stays what the checkpoint expects. Needs a pixel-coordinate lookup (heliographic \u2192 pixel, via SunPy WCS on the frame's timestamp) that I don't see implemented yet in `helio.py`. Downside: blurrier effective resolution on the zoomed region.\n\n**C. True crop at a smaller `img_size`.** Shrinks the search space the most, but per the finding above, the pretrained backbone was never trained on this frequency basis for a smaller grid \u2014 expect it to need more warmup/fine-tuning to adapt, and it's the option most likely to fight your LoRA-only budget. I'd rank this last given 45 samples.\n\n**D. Coordinates as a scalar fed into the head (not the backbone).** Technically trivial (concatenate lat/lon into the pooled embedding before `head_linear`), but flagging a problem: raw latitude's sign *is* hemisphere, so this reintroduces \u2014 and sharpens \u2014 exactly the leak you excluded hemisphere to avoid. I'd avoid this one for a chirality task specifically.\n\nMy instinct is **A**, since it's the smallest change to the pretrained model and cleanly separates \"where to look\" from \"what to conclude.\" Want me to check what coordinate fields are actually available in your original filament source before committing to one?",
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "25c7c412-0b2f-4902-85ec-9c0d12649eca",
      "time": 1789583442.2828436
    },
    {
      "time": 1789583746.3893995,
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "kwebster",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "f39f2197-fe0e-4b4c-a713-92fdc24440cf",
      "body": "The location-prior 14th input channel is a good idea, but I'm not sure if I want to go with it yet. I'm considering going through the whole fine-tuning process with the full disk images and then maybe going back to add it in. What do you think?",
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
