            ],
            "kind": "read",
            "raw_output": "1\t# Adapting the Template for Your Own Task\n2\t\n3\tThis guide walks you through copying the template fine-tuning app and wiring it to a new\n4\tdownstream task. Follow the steps in order \u2014 each one builds on the previous.\n5\t\n6\tThe template task is **solar flare intensity regression** (predicting peak GOES X-ray flux\n7\tfrom SDO image stacks). The numbered scripts and notebooks in this folder give you working\n8\texamples of every step.\n9\t\n10\t**The one rule:** everything generic lives in `workshop_infrastructure/` and is *imported*,\n11\tnever copied. Your app owns only what is specific to your science. If you find yourself\n12\tcopying a file out of `workshop_infrastructure/`, stop \u2014 that is the thing this layout\n13\texists to prevent.\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Overview: what the template gives you\n18\t\n19\t```\n20\tdownstream_apps/template/\n21\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 configs/config_script.yaml       \u2190 single source of truth for all parameters\n22\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 configs.py                       \u2190 FlareDataConfig: the ~4 task-specific config fields\n23\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb\n24\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 1_baseline_template.ipynb\n25\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\n26\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 3_finetune_template_1D.py        \u2190 runnable training script (derived from notebook 2)\n27\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 ADAPTING.md                      \u2190 this file\n28\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 datasets/\n29\t\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 template_dataset.py          \u2190 FlareDSDataset (extends HelioNetCDFDataset)\n30\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 lightning_modules/\n31\t\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 pl_simple_baseline.py        \u2190 FlareLightningModule (Lightning wrapper)\n32\t\u251c\u2500\u2500 metrics/\n33\t\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 template_metrics.py          \u2190 FlareMetrics (loss + evaluation metrics)\n34\t\u2514\u2500\u2500 models/\n35\t    \u2514\u2500\u2500 simple_baseline.py           \u2190 RegressionFlareModel (linear baseline)\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tAnd what it *imports* rather than owning:\n39\t\n40\t| From `workshop_infrastructure/` | What it gives you |\n41\t|---|---|\n42\t| `configs.py` | `DataConfig`, `TrainingConfig`, `ModelConfig`, `load_config()` \u2014 the whole config layer |\n43\t| `datasets/helio.py` | `HelioNetCDFDataset` \u2014 NetCDF loading, local + S3, normalization, frame sampling |\n44\t| `datasets/builders.py` | `build_helio_dataloaders()` \u2014 maps your config onto ~20 dataset arguments |\n45\t| `models/finetune_models.py` | `HelioSpectformer1D` / `HelioSpectformer2D` \u2014 backbone plus a configurable head |\n46\t| `utils.py` | `build_scalers()`, `apply_peft_lora()`, `load_pretrained_weights()`, S3 checkpoint upload |\n47\t\n48\t---\n49\t\n50\t## Step 1 \u2014 Copy the template folder\n51\t\n52\t```bash\n53\tcp -r downstream_apps/template downstream_apps/your_task\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\tThen rename the classes that have \"Flare\" or \"Template\" in their names (Steps 2\u20135 will\n57\ttell you exactly which ones to change).\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## Step 2 \u2014 Declare your task's config fields (`configs.py`)\n62\t\n63\t**File to edit:** `configs.py`.\n64\t\n65\tThis file is short by design. It subclasses the shared `DataConfig` with the handful of\n66\tfields your task needs, and binds `load_config` to that subclass:\n67\t\n68\t```python\n69\t@dataclass\n70\tclass YourDataConfig(DataConfig):\n71\t    your_catalog_path: str = \"\"\n72\t    your_label_column: str = \"flux\"\n73\t\n74\t    # Any field holding a filesystem path must join this list, so relative values in the\n75\t    # YAML resolve against the config file's directory instead of the working directory.\n76\t    PATH_FIELDS: ClassVar[tuple[str, ...]] = DataConfig.PATH_FIELDS + (\"your_catalog_path\",)\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tload_your_config = partial(load_config, data_cls=YourDataConfig)\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tYou inherit every generic field \u2014 paths, channels, temporal sampling, all the S3 settings,\n83\t`max_samples` \u2014 without maintaining a copy of them.\n84\t\n85\tTwo behaviors worth knowing:\n86\t\n87\t- **Unknown keys are an error.** If the YAML has a key with no matching field, `load_config()`\n88\t  raises and lists the valid names. So add the field here *first*, then the YAML key.\n89\t- **Paths resolve relative to the config file**, and `~`/`$VARS` are expanded. Never commit an\n90\t  absolute path into someone else's home directory.\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## Step 3 \u2014 Define your dataset (`datasets/`)\n95\t\n96\t**File to edit:** `datasets/template_dataset.py` \u2192 rename to `your_task_dataset.py`.\n97\t\n98\t`FlareDSDataset` extends `HelioNetCDFDataset` (in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`).\n99\tThe base class handles:\n100\t- Loading NetCDF files from local disk or S3\n101\t- Signum-log normalization per channel\n102\t- Input frame sampling and validity filtering\n103\t\n104\tYou only need to add your task-specific logic in the subclass:\n105\t\n106\t| What to override | Why |\n107\t|---|---|\n108\t| `__init__` | Accept your catalog/label source; pass everything else up via `super().__init__(**kwargs)` |\n109\t| `__getitem__` | Call `super().__getitem__()` to get the image stack, then attach your label |\n110\t\n111\t> **Normalization: know which space you are in.** The dataset applies signum-log\n112\t> compression *and then* a per-channel z-score, so \"undoing the transform\" is ambiguous.\n113\t> `scaler.inverse_transform()` undoes only the z-score, landing in **signum-log** space \u2014\n114\t> that is what `destandardize_channels()` gives the linear baseline, and it is usually what\n115\t> you want as model input, since raw values span many orders of magnitude.\n116\t> `dataset.inverse_transform_data()` undoes **both** stages and returns true **physical**\n117\t> units (DN, Gauss), which is what you want for plotting or a physical-space loss.\n118\t> The full picture is in the \"THE THREE SPACES\" block in\n119\t> `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`.\n120\t\n121\tIf your task supplies its own labels (rather than predicting future SDO frames), set\n122\t`kwargs.setdefault(\"load_forecast_frames\", False)` before calling `super().__init__()`, as\n123\t`FlareDSDataset` does. That stops the loader from fetching future frames it will never use \u2014\n124\tworth roughly 1 GB of S3 traffic per sample.\n125\t\n126\t**Key YAML keys that feed into the dataset** (all under `data:`):\n127\t```yaml\n128\tdata:\n129\t  train_data_path: ...           # CSV index of NetCDF files (timestep, path, present)\n130\t  valid_data_path: ...\n131\t  channels: [...]                # Which SDO channels to load\n132\t  time_delta_input_minutes: [0]  # Temporal offsets for input frames\n133\t  time_delta_target_minutes: 60  # Step size between forecast frames\n134\t  s3_anon: true                  # true = public bucket; false = IAM credentials\n135\t  s3_mode: download              # download | simplecache | stream\n136\t  s3_cache_dir: ~/surya_ws_cache # Required unless s3_mode is \"stream\"\n137\t  max_samples: null              # Cap for quick experiments\n138\t```\n139\t\n140\t### Reading from S3\n141\t\n142\tThe pre-built indices in `data/indices/` point at `s3://nasa-surya-bench/...`, so `s3_mode`\n143\tand `s3_cache_dir` decide how your data actually arrives:\n144\t\n145\t| `s3_mode` | Behavior | Needs `s3_cache_dir`? |\n146\t|---|---|---|\n147\t| `download` (default) | Fetches each whole file into the cache with parallel multipart, then opens it locally. **Recommended** \u2014 NetCDF/HDF5 needs random seeks that streaming cannot serve. | Yes |\n148\t| `simplecache` | fsspec read-through cache. | Yes |\n149\t| `stream` | Reads directly from S3, nothing written to disk. Works, but measured ~9x slower than `download` on a full SDO frame. Use only when disk space is the binding constraint. | No |\n150\t\n151\tThere is deliberately no default cache directory: each SDO file is ~1 GB, so the right\n152\tlocation depends on your machine. Budget ~1 GB \u00d7 the number of unique timesteps. If your\n153\tindex has `s3://` paths and no cache directory is set, dataset construction fails\n154\timmediately with a suggested path \u2014 not twenty minutes into training.\n155\t\n156\t`--s3-cache-dir` overrides it per machine without editing the committed config.\n157\t\n158\t---\n159\t\n160\t## Step 4 \u2014 Define your metrics (`metrics/`)\n161\t\n162\t**File to edit:** `metrics/template_metrics.py` \u2192 rename to `your_task_metrics.py`.\n163\t\n164\t`FlareMetrics` defines four metric sets selected by the `mode` argument at construction:\n165\t\n166\t| Mode | Purpose | Backpropagates? |\n167\t|---|---|---|\n168\t| `\"train_loss\"` | Loss that drives weight updates; logged as `train_loss` | Yes |\n169\t| `\"val_loss\"` | **Logged as `val_loss` \u2014 the quantity `ModelCheckpoint` monitors** | No |\n170\t| `\"train_metrics\"` | Extra metrics logged during training | No |\n171\t| `\"val_metrics\"` | Metrics logged at validation, for reporting only | No |\n172\t\n173\tEach method returns `(dict[str, Tensor], list[float])`: a dict of named metric tensors\n174\tand a list of weights for combining multiple loss terms.\n175\t\n176\tThe dict keys become the metric names in WandB and CSV logs.\n177\t\n178\t> **Which metric selects checkpoints.** `val_loss` does \u2014 not `val_metrics`. The names\n179\t> invite the opposite guess, so it is worth stating plainly: `val_metrics` is logged for\n180\t> reporting and has no effect on which checkpoint is kept.\n181\t>\n182\t> `FlareMetrics.val_loss` delegates to `train_loss` by default, so out of the box the\n183\t> monitored quantity has the same form as the training objective and the two cannot drift\n184\t> apart by accident. **Override `val_loss` in your metrics class** when your task needs a\n185\t> different validation objective \u2014 that is the intended hook, and you should not need to\n186\t> touch `lightning_modules/`.\n187\t>\n188\t> The key is optional: `FlareLightningModule` falls back to `train_loss` if a metrics dict\n189\t> has no `\"val_loss\"` entry.\n190\t\n191\t---\n192\t\n193\t## Step 5 \u2014 Define your model head (`models/`)\n194\t\n195\tFor 1D output tasks (regression, classification): use `HelioSpectformer1D` from\n196\t`workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py`. It wraps the Surya backbone with a\n197\tconfigurable pooling head and a linear output layer.\n198\t\n199\tFor 2D output tasks (pixel-level prediction): use `HelioSpectformer2D`.\n200\t\n201\tThe head is fully configured from the YAML `model:` section \u2014 you usually don't need to\n202\ttouch the model code at all, just adjust the config:\n203\t\n204\t```yaml\n205\tmodel:\n206\t  pooling: class_token        # class_token | global_average | global_max | attention | transformer\n207\t  penultimate_linear_layer: true\n208\t  dropout: 0.2\n209\t  freeze_backbone: false\n210\t  use_lora: true\n211\t  lora_config:\n212\t    r: 8\n213\t    lora_alpha: 8\n214\t    ...\n215\t```\n216\t\n217\t`use_lora` and `freeze_backbone` together select the fine-tuning regime:\n218\t\n219\t| `use_lora` | `freeze_backbone` | Regime |\n220\t|---|---|---|\n221\t| `true` | \u2014 | LoRA adapters on attention + FFN (default; small trainable count) |\n222\t| `false` | `true` | Linear probe \u2014 only the head trains |\n223\t| `false` | `false` | Full fine-tuning of all 366M parameters |\n224\t\n225\tThe training script prints the trainable/total parameter count so you can confirm which\n226\tregime you actually got.\n227\t\n228\tIf your task needs a custom head (e.g. multi-head output, auxiliary losses), create a new\n229\tclass in `models/` following the `RegressionFlareModel` pattern in `simple_baseline.py`.\n230\t\n231\t---\n232\t\n233\t## Step 6 \u2014 Wire it together in the training script\n234\t\n235\t**File to edit:** `3_finetune_template_1D.py`.\n236\t\n237\tOnly two of its four functions have task-specific content:\n238\t\n239\t| Function | What it does | What to change |\n240\t|---|---|---|\n241\t| `build_datasets` | Calls `build_helio_dataloaders()` | Swap `FlareDSDataset` for your subclass and replace the task-specific kwargs below it |\n242\t| `build_model` | Builds `HelioSpectformer1D`, loads weights, applies LoRA / freezing | Usually nothing \u2014 driven by `cfg.model` |\n243\t| `build_trainer` | Loggers, checkpointing, Lightning Trainer | Nothing |\n244\t| `main` | Calls the above in order | Nothing |\n245\t\n246\t`build_datasets` should stay this short \u2014 everything generic is already handled:\n247\t\n248\t```python\n249\tdef build_datasets(cfg):\n250\t    return build_helio_dataloaders(\n251\t        cfg,\n252\t        YourDataset,\n253\t        # \u2193 only your task's kwargs belong here\n254\t        your_catalog_path=cfg.data.your_catalog_path,\n255\t        label_transform=_your_label_transform,\n256\t    )\n257\t```\n258\t\n259\tAlso change the `load_flare_config` import to your own `load_your_config`.\n260\t\n261\t---\n262\t\n263\t## Step 7 \u2014 Edit `config_script.yaml`\n264\t\n265\tThis is the only file you need to edit between experiments. The sections map directly\n266\tto the dataclasses:\n267\t\n268\t| YAML section | Python dataclass | Accessed via |\n269\t|---|---|---|\n270\t| `data:` | your `DataConfig` subclass | `cfg.data.*` |\n271\t| `model:` | `ModelConfig` | `cfg.model.*` |\n272\t| `model.pretrained_path` | `ModelConfig.pretrained_path` | `cfg.model.pretrained_path` |\n273\t| `model.lora_config:` | `LoraAdapterConfig` | `cfg.model.lora_config.*` |\n274\t| `model.time_embedding:` | `TimeEmbeddingConfig` | `cfg.model.time_embedding.*` |\n275\t| `training:` | flat fields on `TrainingConfig` | `cfg.learning_rate`, `cfg.batch_size`, \u2026 |\n276\t| `output:` | `OutputConfig` | `cfg.output.*` |\n277\t| `logging:` | flat fields on `TrainingConfig` | `cfg.wandb_project`, `cfg.wandb_entity` |\n278\t\n279\t---\n280\t\n281\t## Reproducibility\n282\t\n283\tTwo runs of the same config produce the same numbers. That is not free \u2014 it is bought by two\n284\tkeys in the `training:` section, and it is worth understanding what they do before you change\n285\tthem.\n286\t\n287\t```yaml\n288\ttraining:\n289\t  seed: 42             # seeds Python, NumPy, torch, every DataLoader worker, and the shuffle\n290\t  deterministic: false # false | warn | true\n291\t```\n292\t\n293\t| `deterministic` | Behavior |\n294\t|---|---|\n295\t| `false` (default) | No guarantees. Two identical runs may disagree. Chosen as the default for throughput. |\n296\t| `warn` | Bit-identical wherever a deterministic CUDA kernel exists \u2014 which is the whole model as shipped \u2014 and a warning naming the op where one does not. Never blocks a run. |\n297\t| `true` | Hard guarantee: an op with no deterministic kernel raises. **Incompatible with `model.learned_flow: true`**, which uses `F.grid_sample` (no deterministic CUDA backward). |\n298\t\n299\tDeterminism costs roughly **+20% of wall time**: on this template (1 epoch, `max_samples: 10`,\n300\tone A100, LoRA) a run takes 159 s with `false` and 190 s with `warn`. That total includes a fixed\n301\t~1.8 GB checkpoint load, so the overhead on the compute alone is proportionally larger \u2014 budget\n302\tmore than 20% on long runs. The default trades reproducibility for that throughput.\n303\t\n304\t**Switch to `warn` whenever you are comparing results.** With `false`, you cannot tell whether a\n305\tchange in your numbers came from your edit or from drift \u2014 this config produced `val_loss`\n306\t0.060782 and 0.051657 on two runs that differed in nothing at all. Any before/after comparison,\n307\tablation, or hyperparameter sweep should use `warn`.\n308\t\n309\tFor a one-off comparison, pass the flag instead of editing the committed config:\n310\t\n311\t```bash\n312\tpython -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D --deterministic warn\n313\t```\n314\t\n315\tNote that `seed` still does its job with determinism off: the data order stays pinned, so a\n316\tdifferent seed still means a genuinely different run.\n317\t\n318\tTwo implementation details you inherit for free, but should not undo:\n319\t\n320\t- **`CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` is set before `import torch`** \u2014 at the top of\n321\t  `3_finetune_template_1D.py` and in the notebooks' first cell. cuBLAS reads it once at\n322\t  initialization, so setting it later silently does nothing. Without it you get a cuBLAS warning\n323\t  on every run.\n324\t- **The train DataLoader gets an explicit `generator` and `worker_init_fn`** (in\n325\t  `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py`). A bare `shuffle=True` seeds its sampler from\n326\t  whatever the global torch RNG state happens to be when the iterator is created \u2014 so any code\n327\t  you add that consumes RNG beforehand would silently reshuffle your epochs. The worker seeder\n328\t  also seeds Python's `random`, because the channel masker in `helio.py` draws from it.\n329\t\n330\t> **Reproducibility is not significance.** With `max_samples: 10` and one epoch the result is\n331\t> still noise. This makes it the *same* noise every time, so that a change in the number can be\n332\t> attributed to your edit rather than to chance. Before drawing any scientific conclusion, raise\n333\t> `max_samples` and vary `seed`.\n334\t\n335\t---\n336\t\n337\t## Quick reference: running the script\n338\t\n339\t```bash\n340\t# Full run (--config defaults to this app's configs/config_script.yaml)\n341\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D\n342\t\n343\t# Quick sanity check\n344\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\n345\t    --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb\n346\t\n347\t# Same config, different machine's scratch space\n348\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\n349\t    --s3-cache-dir /scratch/$USER/helio_cache\n350\t\n351\t# Reproducible run, for comparing a change against a baseline (~20% slower)\n352\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\n353\t    --deterministic warn\n354\t```\n355\t\n356\tSet `max_samples: 10` in the YAML while developing \u2014 it limits the dataset so data loading\n357\tis fast without changing anything else.\n358\t\n359\t---\n360\t\n361\t## Checklist: files you should have edited\n362\t\n363\tIf you touched anything outside this list, ask whether it belongs in\n364\t`workshop_infrastructure/` instead:\n365\t\n366\t- [ ] `configs.py` \u2014 your `DataConfig` subclass (~15 lines)\n367\t- [ ] `configs/config_script.yaml` \u2014 your paths and hyperparameters\n368\t- [ ] `datasets/your_task_dataset.py` \u2014 your labels\n369\t- [ ] `metrics/your_task_metrics.py` \u2014 your loss and evaluation metrics\n370\t- [ ] `3_finetune_template_1D.py` \u2014 the two imports and the kwargs in `build_datasets`\n371\t- [ ] `models/` \u2014 only if you need a custom head\n372\t",
